+**All-legal measure.** On the 192 development twin boards of the stage (96 per idea) that have at least one verified-good legal move (boards with none are excluded before testing and counted), take the fly's top-scoring moves within the tie tolerance. The same all-move scores give the offset-cancelled pairs. Good means a capture that is good under the protocol's combined rule; a quiet move is not good. King moves and moves the checker cannot certify are unknown. The board scores the share of tied top moves that are good. A board whose tied top set contains an unknown move is an unknown board. **Every eligible board stays in the denominator.** When the diary arm is compared with another arm, the comparison uses the worst case for the diary: the diary arm's unknown boards score 0 and the other arm's unknown boards score 1. The bootstrap lower limit of that worst-case paired difference must be above 0; the version with unknown boards left out is reported beside it. Reason: leaving unknowns out separately in each arm can change the denominators and make an advantage out of nothing (90 of 183 against 90 of 192). If more than 5% of eligible boards are unknown in any arm, the measure is inconclusive and so is the stage.
 
